  > INVENTORY

    The `winslot` command. Run it with no arguments for its spellbook:
      winslot vm list                  who exists, who sleeps
      winslot vm create NAME           summon a new Windows VM (a "license slot")
      winslot vm decommission NAME     put one to sleep (its identity is kept)
      winslot vm recreate NAME         wake it on a fresh disk, same identity
      winslot vm certify NAME          renew its SSH host certificate
      winslot image build / import     forge a new golden Windows image

  > READ INSCRIPTION ON WALL

    I.   A Windows VM's identity (UUID, MAC, disk serial/WWN, machine type, CPU
         model) is what its license is bound to. NEVER `virsh undefine` a winslot VM.
         Decommission it instead. The identity is the license.
    II.  Golden images are built, never patched. Need newer Windows or newer
         software? Build a new image, recreate the VMs onto it.
    III. Every VM's SSH host key was learned over its serial port at first
         boot, before anyone connected, and signed by the host CA. Admins
         trust that CA with one line. If someone ever asks you to "just
         accept the new host key", be suspicious and ask why.
    IV.  The root password on the console is the break-glass key. Whoever can
         see the console owns this box anyway. Root never logs in over SSH.
         Daily work: ssh as tmra-admin, then sudo. The prompt is redrawn at
         every boot (winslot-console.service). Changed root's password by
         hand? The console will admit it no longer knows it. Want a fresh
         one shown there? winslot host console --new-password
    V.   Snapshot before you change anything. Rolling back is cheap; regret
         is not.

